questions.question:
Given a 128-bit header split into hi and lo, pack four fields into non-overlapping bit ranges: A (bits 0-7), B (bits 8-19), C (bits 32-47) in lo, and D (bits 80-111) in hi. Implement:

void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo);

Use only bitwise operations (shifts, AND, OR, NOT); no loops or conditionals. Provide the function signature, a brief justification, plus 1-2 quick examples?

questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit bit packing, non-overlapping fields, and in-place updates without loops. It exercises careful mask calculation and cross-boundary writes.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and clearing specific ranges
- Cross-boundary placement across lo/hi
- Safe in-place updates without branching
- Endianness-agnostic bit numbering

## Code Example
```c
void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFF) | (((uint64_t)0xFFF) << 8) | (((uint64_t)0xFFFF) << 32);
  uint64_t hi_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFFFFFFFF) << 16;
  *out_lo = (lo & ~lo_mask) | (uint64_t)A | (((uint64_t)B) << 8) | (((uint64_t)C) << 32);
  *out_hi = (hi & ~hi_mask) | (((uint64_t)D) << 16);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if ranges could overlap and you needed deterministic behavior?
- How would you validate correctness across all inputs in a unit test?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced 128-bit bit-twiddling, boundary shifts, and correctness under rotation.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Rotation across a 64-bit boundary
- Handling n in [0,127] with minimal branching in production

## Code Example
```javascript
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
  n &= 127; // modulo 128
  if (n < 64){
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (64 - n));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << n) | (x.hi >> (64 - n));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned k = n - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
    uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you make it fully branchless with masks?
- How would you adapt for different word sizes or endianness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform parallel, lane-specific bit manipulation on a 128-bit value without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Video: decompose 128-bit into four 32-bit words; W0 is lo, W3 is hi
- Per-lane rotation: Ri = (Wi << mi) | (Wi >> ((32 - mi) & 31)); mi = ni & 31
- Reassemble lanes in the same order; handle edge cases with safe shifts

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 rotateWords128(uint128 x, unsigned n0, unsigned n1, unsigned n2, unsigned n3) {
  unsigned m0 = n0 & 31, m1 = n1 & 31, m2 = n2 & 31, m3 = n3 & 31;
  uint32_t w0 = (uint32_t)(x.lo);
  uint32_t w1 = (uint32_t)(x.lo >> 32);
  uint32_t w2 = (uint32_t)(x.hi);
  uint32_t w3 = (uint32_t)(x.hi >> 32);
  uint32_t r0 = (w0 << m0) | (w0 >> ((32 - m0) & 31));
  uint32_t r1 = (w1 << m1) | (w1 >> ((32 - m1) & 31));
  uint32_t r2 = (w2 << m2) | (w2 >> ((32 - m2) & 31));
  uint32_t r3 = (w3 << m3) | (w3 >> ((32 - m3) & 31));
  uint128 z;
  z.lo = ((uint64_t)r1 << 32) | r0;
  z.hi = ((uint64_t)r3 << 32) | r2;
  return z;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 64-bit lanes in a 256-bit value? 
- What are performance trade-offs vs. a more generic byte-wise permute?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply bit-dilation technique and Morton code basics, a common need in graphics or spatial indexing.

## Key Concepts

- Bit spreading (dilate) with masks
- Interleaving two bit streams into a 64-bit value
- No-loops approach with fixed masks

## Code Example

```c
uint64_t morton2D64(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  uint64_t dx = x;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dx = (dx | (dx << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  uint64_t dy = y;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  dy = (dy | (dy << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;

  return dx | (dy << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to 3D Morton code with x,y,z?
- What are performance considerations on 64-bit vs 32-bit platforms and compiler optimizations?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform 128-bit two's complement negation and bitwise combination without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit arithmetic via hi/lo halves
- Two's complement negation with carry propagation
- Bitwise AND across full 128-bit value

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestBit128(uint128 x){
  uint128 neg;
  neg.hi = ~x.hi;
  neg.lo = ~x.lo;
  // add 1 to lo, propagate carry
  neg.lo += 1;
  neg.hi += (neg.lo == 0);
  return (uint128){ .hi = x.hi & neg.hi, .lo = x.lo & neg.lo };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test corner cases where trailing zeros span both halves?
- How would you adapt for a wider 256-bit type?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design a deterministic, branchless 128-bit bit-permutation using fixed masks, a common pattern in high-performance bit-twiddling tasks relevant to databases and graphics.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit word with two 64-bit halves
- Nibble-wise bit permutation via fixed masks
- Branchless, mask-and-shift composition

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 galacticShuffle128(uint128 x) {
  const uint64_t M0 = 0x1111111111111111ULL;
  const uint64_t M1 = 0x2222222222222222ULL;
  const uint64_t M2 = 0x4444444444444444ULL;
  const uint64_t M3 = 0x8888888888888888ULL;

  uint64_t lo0 = x.lo & M0;
  uint64_t lo1 = x.lo & M1;
  uint64_t lo2 = x.lo & M2;
  uint64_t lo3 = x.lo & M3;

  uint64_t hi0 = x.hi & M0;
  uint64_t hi1 = x.hi & M1;
  uint64_t hi2 = x.hi & M2;
  uint64_t hi3 = x.hi & M3;

  uint128 r;
  r.lo = lo0 | (lo2 >> 1) | (lo1 << 1) | lo3;
  r.hi = hi0 | (hi2 >> 1) | (hi1 << 1) | hi3;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to a 256-bit type with the same nibble-permutation rule?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures (SIMD lanes, interleaving with memory)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft fixed, branchless bit-twiddling routines across 128‑bit values, a common requirement in high‑performance bitmap or Bloom filter code. It exposes knowledge of SWAR reductions and careful handling of two 64‑bit lanes.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD within a register) reductions
- Handling 128-bit as two 64-bit halves
- Fixed-mask sequences and non-branching sums

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned hi = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned lo = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type?  - Can you prove correctness with a small formal argument or tests?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates non-branching, low-level bit-twiddling mastery and tests the ability to work with 128-bit data using traditional C structures.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount algorithm
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves
- Non-branching reduction using masks and shifts

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) + ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    x = (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) + ((x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL);
    return (unsigned int)x;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
    return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests advanced bit manipulation skills, specifically parallel bit extraction (PEXT-like operations) across 128-bit values. It evaluates the candidate's ability to design branch-free, constant-time operations that scale—skills essential for performance-critical code, cryptography, and systems programming.

## Key Concepts

- **Parallel Bit Extraction (PEXT):** Compact bits from a source value based on a mask, packing them contiguously from the LSB
- **Mask-Driven Bit-Slicing:** Process 128-bit data split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Branch-Free Design:** Use only bitwise operations and shifts—no loops or conditionals
- **Logarithmic Compression:** O(log n) complexity via fixed mask/shift sequences

## Code Example

```cpp
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 compress128(uint128 x, uint128 mask) {
    uint64_t m = mask.lo;
    uint64_t d = x.lo;

    // Parallel extract bits from lo half
    d = (d & m) | ((x.hi & (m >> 64)) << 64);

    // Compression via perfect shuffle and merge
    uint64_t t0 = (d & 0x5555555555555555) | ((d & 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA) >> 1);
    uint64_t t1 = ((d & 0x5555555555555555) << 1) | ((d & 0xAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA) >> 2);
    d = (t0 & 0x3333333333333333) | ((t1 & 0xCCCCCCCCCCCCCCCC) >> 2);

    // Final compression...
    return (uint128){d >> 32, d & 0xFFFFFFFF};
}
```

**Note:** The complete implementation uses a multi-stage logarithmic compression scheme that progressively narrows the bit spacing until only the selected bits remain contiguous.

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes fluency with SWAR bit-twiddling and 128-bit composition, ensuring branchless correctness.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit SWAR reversal
- 128-bit result from {hi: rev64(lo), lo: rev64(hi)}
- Mask/shift sequences to flip bit blocks without branches

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t rev64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) | (x << 32);
  return x;
}

uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = rev64(x.lo);
  r.lo = rev64(x.hi);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test edge alignment and performance implications?
- Could this approach be generalized to arbitrary widths with minimal changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates mastery of fixed-popcount permutations. Tests carry propagation and ability to explain a one-shot solution without loops.

## Key Concepts
- Snoob algorithm for next with same popcount
- Carry ripple and bitwise masks
- Edge-case handling for zero or maximal permutations

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t nextBitPermutation64(uint64_t x) {
    // Assumes x != 0 and not the maximal permutation for its popcount
    uint64_t smallest = x & -x;
    uint64_t ripple = x + smallest;
    uint64_t ones = x ^ ripple;
    ones = (ones >> 2) / smallest;
    return ripple | ones;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to arbitrary bit-widths?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based generator in terms of performance and readability.
